Inheritance: Possible problems in changing a Typed Structures member function signature. See also:

How do I safely change a Type Structure?
 TypeDefine( "ABC::Base" ) {
   Greeting  = Func( Self, ) { Print( "Hello" );  };
   Speak     = Func( Self, ) { Self.Greeting( );  };
   Members() {};
 };

 TypeDefine( "ABC::Spanish" ) {
   Greeting  = Func( Self ) { Print( "Hola" ) };
   Members( "ABC::Base" ) {};
 };

 ABC::Spanish().Speak();
Now, say we need to refactor Func: Greeting() to take in an argument

 TypeDefine( "ABC::Base" ) {
   Greeting  = Func( Self, Greeting := "" ) { Print( Greeting );                       };
   Speak     = Func( Self, )                { Self.Greeting( Greeting := "Hello" );    };
   Members() {};
 };

 TypeDefine( "ABC::Spanish" ) {
   Greeting  = Func( Self ) { Print( "Hola" ) };
   Members( "ABC::Base" ) {};
 };
And we execute the function as below (it will redbox)

 ABC::Spanish().Speak();
We can (sort of) Avoid this by using an Interface, in which case it will redbox during Lint

