The Self Arg & Lambda Functions
The argument "Self" in a typed structure member function is not a normal function argument. You can see it in the debugger, and you may even use it in a Print() statement. But it doesn't behave in other ways like a normal argument, noticeable when you try to use it within a Lambda function.

Lambda functions are another type of weird beast in Slang. They have a peculiar way of capturing a closure environment. And this closure will not find "Self" among local variables. Following is a snippet of code from Test: Typed Structure Self Lamb, demonstrating what not to do:


 Create Bad Lambda = Func( Self, String( Arg 1 ) )
 Returns( Slang() )
 {
     // The returned lambda is broken and won't work.
     // "Self" is not found when the lambda is executed.
     Return( Lambda( String( x ) ) Sprintf( "%s - %s - %s", Self[ "Property 1" ], Arg 1, x ) );
 };
When you run the above code, and attempt to use the lambda function, you will get an error. In fact, if you Lint your _TYPE script, you will see that Lint thinks that the lambda function is attempting to use an "uninitialized variable Self".


 Bad Lambda = ts.Create Bad Lambda( "bad" );
 @Bad Lambda( "123" );
The usual practice is to assign "Self" to a local variable, and then capture this local variable in the lambda's closure:


 Create Good Lambda = Func( Self, String( Arg 1 ) )
 Returns( Slang() )
 {
     This = Self; // Developers understand this as a pattern to workaround this issue
     fn = Lambda( x ) Sprintf( "%s - %s - %s", This.Property 1, Arg 1, x );
     Return( fn );
 };
Note that typed structures are not COW (copy-on-write), unlike Structure and other container types. By default, a typed structure class is not reference-counted. So the above local assignment will actually create a copy of the typed structure. Thus the returned lambda will continue to yield the same results, even if you later change the original typed structure to have a different value in its "Property 1". See this copy behavior confirmed in Test: Typed Structure Self Lamb. If your class is reference counted (via the use of _RefCount), then that won't be the case.

